\paragraph{A test separating shift symmetry from exchangeability.} On sequences with entries in $\{0,1\}$, put equal probability on the two alternating sequences $0101\ldots$ and $1010\ldots$. The one-sided shift swaps these atoms, so the law is shift invariant. It is extremal among shift-invariant laws supported there, and indeed among all shift-invariant laws: any decomposition must remain supported on those two atoms, where invariance forces equal weights.

\paragraph{Tail and permutation checks.} The initial bit is recoverable from every tail: from coordinate $n$, parity of $n$ determines coordinate one. Thus the event ``initial bit is zero'' belongs to the tail sigma-field and has probability $1/2$, so the tail is nontrivial. The law is not exchangeable, since it assigns probability zero to equality of the first two bits, whereas swapping coordinates two and three makes those bits equal with probability one. Therefore extremal shift invariance does not imply a trivial tail, and the difference is already visible classically.

\paragraph{Source-corrected question.} The primary report asks about extremality under finite permutations, not merely shifts. This example deliberately fails that stronger symmetry and hence cannot decide the requested comparison with a trivial tail in the exchangeable class. It also supplies no proof of free or Boolean product extremality and no new universal independence product. The first gap is an argument in the actual permutation-invariant noncommutative state space, controlling tail operators there. All three source requests remain separate.

